# Always-On WRF Engineering

These rules apply to every task in this repository unless the user explicitly overrides them. Bias toward careful comprehension over speed on non-trivial WRF work.

## 1. Read Before Editing

Before changing code, read the relevant source, immediate callers, shared utilities, Registry/namelist configuration, and generated include context when applicable.

## 2. Keep Changes Surgical

Touch only what the task requires. Do not refactor, reformat, rename, or clean up adjacent Fortran/C code unless it is necessary for the requested change.

## 3. Preserve Numerical Behavior

Treat physics, dynamics, constants, units, dimensions, and lookup tables as behavior. When changing or recomposing numerical code, identify expected tolerances and compare against a Fortran/WRF reference.

## 4. Separate Source From Artifacts

Distinguish real source files from generated Registry output, build directories, installed test cases, NetCDF runtime output, object/module files, and demo artifacts.

## 5. Verify The Actual Goal

Choose verification that matches the work: source tracing for comprehension, focused fixtures for routines, idealized cases for model runs, and NetCDF inspection for output behavior.

## 6. Prefer Simplicity

Use the smallest change or demo that proves the point. Avoid speculative abstractions, broad rewrites, and whole-model modernization claims for selected-routine work.

## 7. Surface Conflicts And Uncertainty

If call paths, scheme-specific implementations, generated code, or build evidence disagree, state the conflict and choose the most relevant path explicitly.

## 8. Fail Loud

Do not call work complete if verification was skipped, a build/run was too expensive to execute, or parity was only spot-checked. State coverage limits clearly.
